---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import StudyNav from "../../components/studies/StudyNav.astro";
import StudyBanner from "../../components/studies/StudyBanner.astro";
import PlanFigure from "../../components/studies/PlanFigure.astro";
import { plans, plan, testfit, finance, usd, num } from "../../data/studies";
import { trinityFigures as F, trinityRelease } from "../../data/trinity";
const floor = plan("peaks-floor-a");
const A = finance.results.A;
const tall = testfit.rise.filter((r) => r.inBaseline);
const peaksMid = testfit.peaks.find((s) => s.residentialFloors === 5)!;
const peaksHigh = testfit.peaks.find((s) => s.residentialFloors === 6)!;
const peaksLow = testfit.peaks.find((s) => s.residentialFloors === 3)!;
const prem25 = finance.premium.find((p) => p.premiumPerBed === 25000)!;
---

<Base
  title="Trinity Residential Design Studies — Ivan Minier"
  theme="studies"
  image="/studies/plans/peaks-floor-a/peaks-floor-a-v1.png"
  description="Independent schematic floor plans, whole-building test fits, and a preliminary development model exploring Trinity Peaks and Trinity Rise. Not UVM designs."
>
  <header class="st-hero wrap-wide">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Portfolio · Built environment · November 2026</p>
      <h1>Trinity Residential Design Studies</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Can the rooms behind the Trinity Redistricting renderings actually be drawn — and do the
        numbers still work when they are?
      </p>
    </div>
    <StudyBanner />
  </header>
  <StudyNav />

  <section class="wrap-wide st-section">
    <div class="st-split">
      <div class="st-prose">
        <span class="st-num">Why these studies exist</span>
        <h2>The proposal had renderings and bed counts, but no floor plans.</h2>
        <p>
          The public proposal (Version {trinityRelease.version}) sets targets of {F.peaksBeds} beds
          for Trinity Peaks and {F.riseBeds} for Trinity Rise, and says plainly that no massing test
          fit, gross floor area, or room inventory exists. These studies are my first attempt to
          fill that gap with original, editable schematic drawings — and to see what they imply
          for height, footprint, and rent.
        </p>
        <p>
          They are a portfolio extension. Nothing here changes the proposal's published figures or
          commitments, and the premium scenario in Study B is a deliberately hypothetical comparison,
          not a recommendation for UVM.
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="st-kpis" aria-label="What the studies produced">
        <div><dt>Original schematic plans</dt><dd>{plans.length}<small>SVG, PNG, PDF, DXF + schedules</small></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Geometry checks</dt><dd>0 errors<small>overlaps, access, doors, beds</small></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Typical Peaks floor</dt><dd>{floor.beds} beds<small>{num(floor.grossArea)} sf · {floor.grossAreaPerBed} sf/bed</small></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Workbook formulas verified</dt><dd>85<small>LibreOffice vs. independent Python</small></dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap-wide st-section">
    <span class="st-num">Two studies</span>
    <div class="st-cols">
      <div>
        <h2>Study A · Trinity Peaks</h2>
        <p>First- and second-year suites using UVM's University Heights as a typology reference: singles and doubles, shared bathrooms, RA rooms, study and common space, and no forced triples.</p>
        <PlanFigure id="peaks-p6" />
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/studies/peaks">Open Study A ↗</a></p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Study B · Trinity Rise</h2>
        <p>Upperclass four-bedroom apartments with private bedrooms, kitchens, and laundry — drawn twice to test whether ensuite bathrooms are worth their cost.</p>
        <PlanFigure id="rise-r4a" />
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/studies/rise">Open Study B ↗</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap-wide st-section">
    <span class="st-num">Findings so far</span>
    <h2>What the drawings say back to the proposal.</h2>
    <div class="st-cols">
      <div class="st-card">
        <h3>Peaks capacity depends on height</h3>
        <p>
          With the typical floor drawn here ({floor.beds} beds), {F.peaksBeds} beds need about
          {peaksHigh.low.barsEquivalent}–{peaksMid.high.barsEquivalent} floor-plate equivalents at
          5–6 residential floors — roughly {Math.round(peaksMid.heightFt.at105)}–{Math.round(peaksHigh.heightFt.at105)} ft
          tall by simple arithmetic, above the 55-ft ceiling. Staying under 55 ft allows about
          {testfit.peaksUnder55["10.5"]} residential floors and roughly doubles the footprint
          ({num(peaksLow.low.footprintSf)}–{num(peaksLow.high.footprintSf)} sf).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="st-card">
        <h3>Rise needs a big floor plate</h3>
        <p>
          With four-bedroom apartments, 300–400 beds at 8–12 stories is reached only by
          {tall.map((t) => `${t.unitsPerFloor} units per floor at ${t.stories} stories (${t.beds} beds)`).join(" or ")}.
          A slender six-unit tower tops out near {testfit.rise.find((r) => r.unitsPerFloor === 6 && r.stories === 12)!.beds} beds at 12 stories.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="st-card">
        <h3>A new building can't be paid for by its own rents alone</h3>
        <p>
          At a sourced per-bed cost benchmark, debt service alone would be about
          {usd(A.debtService / 300)} per bed per year if the building were fully debt-financed on its
          own — more than UVM's current academic-year University Heights suite-single rate ($12,090,
          room only). Universities usually finance housing across the whole system, with reserves
          and gifts, so this is a project-level test, not a verdict: it shows why system financing,
          equity, or lower cost matter.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="st-card">
        <h3>Premium features are cheap per month, but not free</h3>
        <p>
          In the hypothetical private scenario, an illustrative $25,000-per-bed premium for ensuite
          baths and finishes needs about {usd(prem25.extraRentPerMonth)} more rent per bed per month
          to keep the same yield. Whether students would pay it is a market question this study
          does not answer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      All findings rest on exploratory dimensions and illustrative financial inputs. They are
      prompts for professional study, not conclusions about the real site.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap-wide st-section">
    <span class="st-num">Deliverables</span>
    <h2>Everything is downloadable and editable.</h2>
    <div class="st-next">
      <a href="/studies/peaks"><strong>Peaks suites + floor</strong><span>4 plans · options compared with University Heights benchmarks</span></a>
      <a href="/studies/rise"><strong>Rise apartments</strong><span>2 plans · shared vs. ensuite bathrooms</span></a>
      <a href="/studies/test-fit"><strong>Whole-building test fits</strong><span>height, floors, footprint, coverage</span></a>
      <a href="/studies/development-model"><strong>Development model</strong><span>Excel workbook · two scenarios · sensitivities</span></a>
      <a href="/studies/method"><strong>Method, sources & assumptions</strong><span>assumption register · revision workflow</span></a>
      <a href="/portfolio/trinity"><strong>Portfolio presentation</strong><span>concise admissions-style summary + PDF</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
